first-class `Stream` value would be, so [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) and this proposal stand or fall together.\n283:One connection: adopting projection semantics pulls [the binary-operator question](plans/questions.md#q2-binary-operators-over-two-multi-valued-expressions-cartesian-zip-or-explicit) (cartesian vs. zip) toward\n284:broadcast, because multidimensional projection and elementwise broadcast are the same tradition.\n285:[That question](plans/questions.md#q2-binary-operators-over-two-multi-valued-expressions-cartesian-zip-or-explicit) and this TODO may be one question.\n629:question; variants cannot avoid it. Tracked as [Q29](plans/questions.md#q29-what-is-the-default-discriminant-convention-for-a-derived-enum-codec).\n1095:Arithmetic on tensors broadcasts, so `$m * 2` is elementwise. That extension is confined to the\n1116:[Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity).\n1182:while behaving like a record, and the multiplicity is invisible at the call site.\n1187:{a:1} | .a =  (1,2)      # -> {a:1}, {a:2}    cartesian\n1219:whether binary operators are cartesian, zipped, or explicit, so it should not be settled alone.\n1236:common is that multiplicity leaked into a position that wanted exactly one value.\n1316:| `[.[] + .[]]` | cartesian, `[2,3,3,4]` | not expressible by accident |\n1799:Not an attempt to settle [Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity)\n1800:in general. Q1 leans evaluation-level -- no `Stream<T>` type constructor, multiplicity born from\n1910:would even mean, is [Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)'s\n1936:([Q35](plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return) again): nothing can\n2185:This settles [Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity), and not by\n2186:picking either of its two named options. Streams are evaluation-level multiplicity, as every\n2188:multiplicity down. `Stream<T>` enters the type grammar as the type *of* effect-layer\n2189:multiplicity: it says \"this expression yields its entries one at a time as evaluation\n2191:cardinality table presenting `Stream<T>` as a type while the layer model treated multiplicity\n2228:the vec-literal and [cartesian-vs-zip](plans/questions.md#q2-binary-operators-over-two-multi-valued-expressions-cartesian-zip-or-explicit)\n2330:running a matcher to a `Result`, and deep patterns -- Q27's decode work, when it has\n","metadata":{"output":"15:**Value layer** means multiplicity is stored *in a value*. An array of three things is one\n18:**Effect layer** means multiplicity is a property of *evaluation*. An expression that yields\n23:a thing\": collect evaluation multiplicity into a value. Reflect is the opposite, taking a\n24:value's contents and turning them back into evaluation multiplicity.\n33:[1,2,3] | map(select(. > 1))     # -> [2,3]     multiplicity became array length\n34:[1,2,3] | .[] | select(. > 1)    # -> 2, 3      multiplicity stayed in the evaluation\n37:Same computation, same values. The only difference is *where the multiplicity lives*.\n61:Layer is about where multiplicity is *stored*. Index versus iterate is about what access a type\n64:If multiplicity in the effect layer is purely an evaluation-time phenomenon, with no stream\n66:the only iterate-only thing, and `Vec` is the only multiplicity-bearing value.\n74:effect multiplicity) and a held value of genuinely unknown extent. See\n106:thing that turns a value into effect-layer multiplicity. Under projection-with-lenses, `.[]` on\n124:effect-layer multiplicity at all. What does? Only things with genuinely unknown extent: I/O,\n155:information.** Ask what converting a `Vec` into effect multiplicity actually buys. Effect-layer\n156:multiplicity exists to describe extent that is not yet known. A `Vec` already knows its extent.\n160:Under this reading, effect multiplicity is *born* from streaming sources whose length has not\n172:It answers [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity). If effect multiplicity is only ever born from I/O and only ever collapses into\n182:first-class `Stream` value would be, so [the streams question](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity) and this proposal stand or fall together.\n283:One connection: adopting projection semantics pulls [the binary-operator question](plans/questions.md#q2-binary-operators-over-two-multi-valued-expressions-cartesian-zip-or-explicit) (cartesian vs. zip) toward\n284:broadcast, because multidimensional projection and elementwise broadcast are the same tradition.\n285:[That question](plans/questions.md#q2-binary-operators-over-two-multi-valued-expressions-cartesian-zip-or-explicit) and this TODO may be one question.\n629:question; variants cannot avoid it. Tracked as [Q29](plans/questions.md#q29-what-is-the-default-discriminant-convention-for-a-derived-enum-codec).\n1095:Arithmetic on tensors broadcasts, so `$m * 2` is elementwise. That extension is confined to the\n1116:[Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity).\n1182:while behaving like a record, and the multiplicity is invisible at the call site.\n1187:{a:1} | .a =  (1,2)      # -> {a:1}, {a:2}    cartesian\n1219:whether binary operators are cartesian, zipped, or explicit, so it should not be settled alone.\n1236:common is that multiplicity leaked into a position that wanted exactly one value.\n1316:| `[.[] + .[]]` | cartesian, `[2,3,3,4]` | not expressible by accident |\n1799:Not an attempt to settle [Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity)\n1800:in general. Q1 leans evaluation-level -- no `Stream<T>` type constructor, multiplicity born from\n1910:would