{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217708962,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-90008a12e5fe5c2c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/plans/questions.md","offset":70,"limit":45},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/plans/questions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n70: consumed exactly once, not a value type. Recorded in\n71: [ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md).\n72: \n73: ### Q2. Binary operators over two multi-valued expressions: cartesian, zip, or explicit?\n74: \n75: Cartesian (jq today), zip\n76: (vectorized, with broadcast), or neither by default with explicit `cross` and `zip`?\n77: \n78: Vec concatenation specifically is decided, without touching the rest of this question. Revised\n79: 2026-08-30 (oddities round, kantord/toylang#97): `+` on two `Vec`s of the same element type now\n80: concatenates them -- the add-trait reading -- so `[1, 2] + [3]` is `[1, 2, 3]`, settling this\n81: half of Q2 in favor of concatenation over cartesian or zip. The named builtin this superseded,\n82: `concat(vv: Vec<Vec<T>>) -> Vec<T>`, existed specifically so adding it would not decide this\n83: question (see [named functions kept an open question\n84: open](../research-log/named-functions-kept-an-open-question-open.md)); it survives under the name\n85: `flatten` for the case `+` cannot cover, an outer `Vec` whose length is not known at the call\n86: site. The general question -- what any *other* operator means when both operands are Vecs --\n87: is still open.\n88: \n89: Composite equality is settled without touching it. `==` on a record or an enum compares\n90: structurally, and is refused outright when the type carries a Vec anywhere inside it, so a\n91: `Vec`-typed record field never quietly acquires whole-value semantics\n92: ([the equality decision](../draft.md#decided-equality-on-a-composite-is-structural-and-stops-at-a-vec)).\n93: \n94: ### Q3. What symbol replaces `=` for the record-forming update?\n95: \n96: ### Q4. Can the type express ordering over heterogeneous streams?\n97: \n98: Subsumes the older cardinality-versus-\n99: order thread, which asked whether the type system should track *how many* values an\n100: expression produces or *in what order* the kinds arrive. Those turned out to be the same\n101: question asked from two sides, so they are tracked here as one. The cardinality half is the\n102: cheaper and more decidable option, and it catches the failure that actually bites, which is\n103: multiplicity leaking into a position wanting exactly one value. The order half is what the\n104: rest of this entry is about.\n105: If a stream is \"some `A`s, then some `B`s\", can the type say so? One approach is *regular\n106: expressions over types*, the same\n107: algebra as string regexes but with types as the alphabet, so a pattern denotes a set of\n108: permitted value-sequences: `Seq<A,B>` = `A* B*`, `Alt<A,B>` = `(A|B)*`, `Star<A>` = `A*`.\n109: Three primitives suffice (Kleene's theorem), it is decidable, and unlike full session types\n110: it needs no *linear types*, a discipline requiring each value be consumed exactly once,\n111: which is powerful but infects the whole system. Unpacking one item is then the **derivative**\n112: of the pattern: given that an `A` was just consumed, what remains? Open: how type tagging is\n113: represented so the runtime and type-level guarantees stay symmetrical.\n114: \n\n(Showing lines 70-114 of 553. Use offset=115 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"consumed exactly once, not a value type. Recorded in\n[ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md).\n\n### Q2. Binary operators over two multi-valued expressions: cartesian, zip, or explicit?\n\nCartesian (jq today), zip\n(vectorized, with broadcast), or neither by default with explicit `cross` and `zip`?\n\nVec concatenation specifically is decided, without touching the rest of this question. Revised\n2026-08-30 (oddities round, kantord/toylang#97): `+` on two `Vec`s of the same element type now\nconcatenates them -- the add-trait reading -- so `[1, 2] + [3]` is `[1, 2, 3]`, settling this\nhalf of Q2 in favor of concatenation over cartesian or zip. The named builtin this superseded,\n`concat(vv: Vec<Vec<T>>) -> Vec<T>`, existed specifically so adding it would not decide this\nquestion (see [named functions kept an open question\nopen](../research-log/named-functions-kept-an-open-question-open.md)); it survives under the name\n`flatten` for the case `+` cannot cover, an outer `Vec` whose length is not known at the call\nsite. The general question -- what any *other* operator means when both operands are Vecs --\nis still open.\n\nComposite equality is settled without touching it. `==` on a record or an enum compares","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/plans/questions.md","text":"consumed exactly once, not a value type. Recorded in\n[ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md).\n\n### Q2. Binary operators over two multi-valued expressions: cartesian, zip, or explicit?\n\nCartesian (jq today), zip\n(vectorized, with broadcast), or neither by default with explicit `cross` and `zip`?\n\nVec concatenation specifically is decided, without touching the rest of this question. Revised\n2026-08-30 (oddities round, kantord/toylang#97): `+` on two `Vec`s of the same element type now\nconcatenates them -- the add-trait reading -- so `[1, 2] + [3]` is `[1, 2, 3]`, settling this\nhalf of Q2 in favor of concatenation over cartesian or zip. The named builtin this superseded,\n`concat(vv: Vec<Vec<T>>) -> Vec<T>`, existed specifically so adding it would not decide this\nquestion (see [named functions kept an open question\nopen](../research-log/named-functions-kept-an-open-question-open.md)); it survives under the name\n`flatten` for the case `+` cannot cover, an outer `Vec` whose length is not known at the call\nsite. The general question -- what any *other* operator means when both operands are Vecs --\nis still open.\n\nComposite equality is settled without touching it. `==` on a record or an enum compares\nstructurally, and is refused outright when the type carries a Vec anywhere inside it, so a\n`Vec`-typed record field never quietly acquires whole-value semantics\n([the equality decision](../draft.md#decided-equality-on-a-composite-is-structural-and-stops-at-a-vec)).\n\n### Q3. What symbol replaces `=` for the record-forming update?\n\n### Q4. Can the type express ordering over heterogeneous streams?\n\nSubsumes the older cardinality-versus-\norder thread, which asked whether the type system should track *how many* values an\nexpression produces or *in what order* the kinds arrive. Those turned out to be the same\nquestion asked from two sides, so they are tracked here as one. The cardinality half is the\ncheaper and more decidable option, and it catches the failure that actually bites, which is\nmultiplicity leaking into a position wanting exactly one value. The order half is what the\nrest of this entry is about.\nIf a stream is \"some `A`s, then some `B`s\", can the type say so? One approach is *regular\nexpressions over types*, the same\nalgebra as string regexes but with types as the alphabet, so a pattern denotes a set of\npermitted value-sequences: `Seq<A,B>` = `A* B*`, `Alt<A,B>` = `(A|B)*`, `Star<A>` = `A*`.\nThree primitives suffice (Kleene's theorem), it is decidable, and unlike full session types\nit needs no *linear types*, a discipline requiring each value be consumed exactly once,\nwhich is powerful but infects the whole system. Unpacking one item is then the **derivative**\nof the pattern: given that an `A` was just consumed, what remains? Open: how type tagging is\nrepresented so the runtime and type-level guarantees stay symmetrical.\n","lineStart":70,"lineEnd":114,"totalLines":553,"truncated":true}},"title":"plans/questions.md","time":{"start":1788217708955,"end":1788217708960}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_05a14cf45001Cfex9hDmlRwxHZ","sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","messageID":"msg_05a14cb4b001YuUM77hW6HPNi6"}}
