{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217717906,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-92bc145c195d6334","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md","limit":40,"offset":1085},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1085: `@f32` is also the second number type. This language commits to `f64` to match JSON, and `@f32`\n1086: is a deliberate lossy exit from that commitment, which is a good reason to make it a visible\n1087: operator rather than something a type inferencer decides.\n1088: \n1089: Nulls are the awkward part, since JSON has one and a dense `f32` buffer does not. **Do not use\n1090: NaN as a sentinel**, because it collides with genuine NaN. Apache Arrow already solved exactly\n1091: this with a separate validity bitmask beside the values buffer, and adopting its layout also buys\n1092: zero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB, and pandas. For a language whose pitch is JSON processing,\n1093: that is a large return on a layout decision that has to be made anyway.\n1094: \n1095: Arithmetic on tensors broadcasts, so `$m * 2` is elementwise. That extension is confined to the\n1096: new type; plain JSON arrays keep erroring exactly as they do now.\n1097: \n1098: TODO (user): stdin and stdout should not require solving generic effect types, because they are\n1099: always string streams and so have no choice of element type. At worst the end of input shows up\n1100: at the type level, and a `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive, working the way `Opt<>` does, would be\n1101: the most trivial effect type: one that promises it is potentially not infinite in time.\n1102: \n1103: RESPONSE (agent). The pragmatic move is right and the bit being asked for already exists, but it\n1104: is named on the wrong side, and the proposal cuts against a leaning that has four arguments\n1105: behind it. Those are three separate things.\n1106: \n1107: **The bit already exists.** In the base-functor table above, `Stream<T> = nuX. 1 + T*X`, and the\n1108: `1 +` is exactly closability: it is the case where the stream stops. So `ClosableStream<T>` is\n1109: `Stream<T>` as already defined. What has no name is the *other* one, `nuX. T*X`, which never\n1110: stops. If a name is wanted for the distinction, it should attach there, because that is the case\n1111: a reduction over the stream can never produce a value for.\n1112: \n1113: **The pragmatic move is right.** stdin and stdout are byte streams with no element type to\n1114: choose, so the machinery a general `Stream<T>` needs is not needed to build them. That is a real\n1115: saving and it is the difference between starting now and waiting on\n1116: [Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity).\n1117: \n1118: **The tension.** Q1 leans evaluation-level, meaning there is no `Stream<T>` *type* at all and\n1119: `Stream` is an effect annotation on an expression. A `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive is a stream\n1120: type, so taken generally it reverses that.\n1121: \n1122: The sidestep that makes it a starting point rather than a reversal: one concrete opaque built-in\n1123: is not a type constructor. A file handle is a value in languages that have no first-class\n1124: streams, and `ClosableStream<Str>` can be that, monomorphic and un-parameterisable, without\n\n(Showing lines 1085-1124 of 2480. Use offset=1125 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"`@f32` is also the second number type. This language commits to `f64` to match JSON, and `@f32`\nis a deliberate lossy exit from that commitment, which is a good reason to make it a visible\noperator rather than something a type inferencer decides.\n\nNulls are the awkward part, since JSON has one and a dense `f32` buffer does not. **Do not use\nNaN as a sentinel**, because it collides with genuine NaN. Apache Arrow already solved exactly\nthis with a separate validity bitmask beside the values buffer, and adopting its layout also buys\nzero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB, and pandas. For a language whose pitch is JSON processing,\nthat is a large return on a layout decision that has to be made anyway.\n\nArithmetic on tensors broadcasts, so `$m * 2` is elementwise. That extension is confined to the\nnew type; plain JSON arrays keep erroring exactly as they do now.\n\nTODO (user): stdin and stdout should not require solving generic effect types, because they are\nalways string streams and so have no choice of element type. At worst the end of input shows up\nat the type level, and a `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive, working the way `Opt<>` does, would be\nthe most trivial effect type: one that promises it is potentially not infinite in time.\n\nRESPONSE (agent). The pragmatic move is right and the bit being asked for already exists, but it\nis named on the wrong side, and the proposal cuts against a leaning that has four arguments","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md","text":"`@f32` is also the second number type. This language commits to `f64` to match JSON, and `@f32`\nis a deliberate lossy exit from that commitment, which is a good reason to make it a visible\noperator rather than something a type inferencer decides.\n\nNulls are the awkward part, since JSON has one and a dense `f32` buffer does not. **Do not use\nNaN as a sentinel**, because it collides with genuine NaN. Apache Arrow already solved exactly\nthis with a separate validity bitmask beside the values buffer, and adopting its layout also buys\nzero-copy interop with Polars, DuckDB, and pandas. For a language whose pitch is JSON processing,\nthat is a large return on a layout decision that has to be made anyway.\n\nArithmetic on tensors broadcasts, so `$m * 2` is elementwise. That extension is confined to the\nnew type; plain JSON arrays keep erroring exactly as they do now.\n\nTODO (user): stdin and stdout should not require solving generic effect types, because they are\nalways string streams and so have no choice of element type. At worst the end of input shows up\nat the type level, and a `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive, working the way `Opt<>` does, would be\nthe most trivial effect type: one that promises it is potentially not infinite in time.\n\nRESPONSE (agent). The pragmatic move is right and the bit being asked for already exists, but it\nis named on the wrong side, and the proposal cuts against a leaning that has four arguments\nbehind it. Those are three separate things.\n\n**The bit already exists.** In the base-functor table above, `Stream<T> = nuX. 1 + T*X`, and the\n`1 +` is exactly closability: it is the case where the stream stops. So `ClosableStream<T>` is\n`Stream<T>` as already defined. What has no name is the *other* one, `nuX. T*X`, which never\nstops. If a name is wanted for the distinction, it should attach there, because that is the case\na reduction over the stream can never produce a value for.\n\n**The pragmatic move is right.** stdin and stdout are byte streams with no element type to\nchoose, so the machinery a general `Stream<T>` needs is not needed to build them. That is a real\nsaving and it is the difference between starting now and waiting on\n[Q1](plans/questions.md#q1-streams-first-class-values-or-evaluation-level-multiplicity).\n\n**The tension.** Q1 leans evaluation-level, meaning there is no `Stream<T>` *type* at all and\n`Stream` is an effect annotation on an expression. A `ClosableStream<Str>` primitive is a stream\ntype, so taken generally it reverses that.\n\nThe sidestep that makes it a starting point rather than a reversal: one concrete opaque built-in\nis not a type constructor. A file handle is a value in languages that have no first-class\nstreams, and `ClosableStream<Str>` can be that, monomorphic and un-parameterisable, without","lineStart":1085,"lineEnd":1124,"totalLines":2480,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788217717897,"end":1788217717904}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The board row jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research isn't in board.yaml. The brief references it as \"the exact question being answered\" but it doesn't exist. That's fine — the question is clear from the issue itself (Q2). I don't need to escalate; the issue gives the exact question. I'll proceed.\n\nLet me understand toylang syntax a bit to write toylang-under-B and toylang-under-C equivalents. Let me look at draft.md around the operator question, and at how the language works. Let me look at the draft.md relevant section and the \"here\" column meaning.\n\nLet me look at draft.md around 1095 and 1180-1320.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a14f347001XHlH1QehRo3O1c","sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","messageID":"msg_05a14e96a001TlpcR4oNupSwWW"}}
