{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217720193,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b452834e6b8decd0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md","limit":70,"offset":1170},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1170: \n1171: ### UNDECIDED: what to call the record-forming update\n1172: \n1173: In jq, `=` is not assignment. Its right-hand side is an ordinary expression, so if it yields\n1174: several values, the whole update yields several results:\n1175: \n1176: ```\n1177: {} | .a = (1,2)        # -> {a:1}, {a:2}      TWO objects, not one object with two values\n1178: ```\n1179: \n1180: That is genuinely useful. It gives config-matrix expansion, variant generation, and\n1181: property-test input enumeration for free. The problem is purely that `=` *looks* like mutation\n1182: while behaving like a record, and the multiplicity is invisible at the call site.\n1183: \n1184: Compounding it, jq's `=` and `|=` disagree about cardinality and say nothing about it:\n1185: \n1186: ```\n1187: {a:1} | .a =  (1,2)      # -> {a:1}, {a:2}    cartesian\n1188: {a:1} | .a |= (.,.+10)   # -> {a:1}           silently keeps only the first\n1189: ```\n1190: \n1191: Options under consideration:\n1192: \n1193: **A. Keep `=`.** Familiar to anyone arriving from jq, with zero migration cost. But it\n1194: preserves exactly the readability problem, and the `=` versus `|=` mismatch stays a trap.\n1195: \n1196: **B. Require `One` on the right, and make forking explicit.** `=` typechecks only when its\n1197: right-hand side yields exactly one value, so the surprising case becomes a compile error. When\n1198: a record is wanted, it is written out:\n1199: \n1200: ```\n1201: db.color = \"red\"                        # ok\n1202: db.color = (\"red\", \"blue\")              # ERROR: expected One<Str>, found 2 values\n1203: (\"red\",\"blue\") as $c | db.color = $c    # explicit; jq already supports this and it reads better\n1204: ```\n1205: \n1206: **C. Two distinct operators.** `=` for the single-valued case, and a visually distinct one for\n1207: the deliberate record, such as `.color =* (\"red\",\"blue\")` or `.color each= (...)`. Keeps both\n1208: without either being silent, at the cost of more surface.\n1209: \n1210: **D. Drop `=` entirely and keep only `|=`.** All updates go through the update operator, and\n1211: records come from an explicit `cross` or `for` construct. Smallest core, largest departure.\n1212: \n1213: **E. Rename to a functional-update keyword.** `db with .color = \"red\"`, in the spirit of record\n1214: update in ML-family languages. Removes the mutation reading, but adds a keyword and does not by\n1215: itself resolve the cardinality question.\n1216: \n1217: Leaning towards B, because it makes the hazard a type error rather than a naming problem, and\n1218: the explicit form already exists and reads better. But this interacts with open question 2,\n1219: whether binary operators are cartesian, zipped, or explicit, so it should not be settled alone.\n1220: \n1221: ## Why cardinality-in-the-type is the safety mechanism\n1222: \n1223: jq's multi-output semantics produce hazards that are all the same shape, namely the right\n1224: feature in the wrong position. Measured against a jq implementation:\n1225: \n1226: ```\n1227: if (true,false) then \"a\" else \"b\" end   -> [\"a\",\"b\"]       BOTH branches executed\n1228: {} | .a = (1,2)                          -> [{a:1},{a:2}]   assignment forked the world\n1229: {a:1} | .a |= (.,.+10)                   -> [{a:1}]         |= silently took only the first\n1230: (1,2) | (., error(\"boom\"))?              -> [1,2]           `?` truncated with no signal\n1231: ```\n1232: \n1233: None of these is an argument against nondeterminism, because each is genuinely useful\n1234: somewhere. Forking on assignment is config-matrix expansion. Both-branches is nondeterministic\n1235: choice. Truncation under `?` is \"take the valid prefix\" of a corrupt stream. What they have in\n1236: common is that multiplicity leaked into a position that wanted exactly one value.\n1237: \n1238: Making cardinality visible turns each into a type error at the point of the mistake:\n1239: \n\n(Showing lines 1170-1239 of 2480. Use offset=1240 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n### UNDECIDED: what to call the record-forming update\n\nIn jq, `=` is not assignment. Its right-hand side is an ordinary expression, so if it yields\nseveral values, the whole update yields several results:\n\n```\n{} | .a = (1,2)        # -> {a:1}, {a:2}      TWO objects, not one object with two values\n```\n\nThat is genuinely useful. It gives config-matrix expansion, variant generation, and\nproperty-test input enumeration for free. The problem is purely that `=` *looks* like mutation\nwhile behaving like a record, and the multiplicity is invisible at the call site.\n\nCompounding it, jq's `=` and `|=` disagree about cardinality and say nothing about it:\n\n```\n{a:1} | .a =  (1,2)      # -> {a:1}, {a:2}    cartesian\n{a:1} | .a |= (.,.+10)   # -> {a:1}           silently keeps only the first\n```","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md","text":"\n### UNDECIDED: what to call the record-forming update\n\nIn jq, `=` is not assignment. Its right-hand side is an ordinary expression, so if it yields\nseveral values, the whole update yields several results:\n\n```\n{} | .a = (1,2)        # -> {a:1}, {a:2}      TWO objects, not one object with two values\n```\n\nThat is genuinely useful. It gives config-matrix expansion, variant generation, and\nproperty-test input enumeration for free. The problem is purely that `=` *looks* like mutation\nwhile behaving like a record, and the multiplicity is invisible at the call site.\n\nCompounding it, jq's `=` and `|=` disagree about cardinality and say nothing about it:\n\n```\n{a:1} | .a =  (1,2)      # -> {a:1}, {a:2}    cartesian\n{a:1} | .a |= (.,.+10)   # -> {a:1}           silently keeps only the first\n```\n\nOptions under consideration:\n\n**A. Keep `=`.** Familiar to anyone arriving from jq, with zero migration cost. But it\npreserves exactly the readability problem, and the `=` versus `|=` mismatch stays a trap.\n\n**B. Require `One` on the right, and make forking explicit.** `=` typechecks only when its\nright-hand side yields exactly one value, so the surprising case becomes a compile error. When\na record is wanted, it is written out:\n\n```\ndb.color = \"red\"                        # ok\ndb.color = (\"red\", \"blue\")              # ERROR: expected One<Str>, found 2 values\n(\"red\",\"blue\") as $c | db.color = $c    # explicit; jq already supports this and it reads better\n```\n\n**C. Two distinct operators.** `=` for the single-valued case, and a visually distinct one for\nthe deliberate record, such as `.color =* (\"red\",\"blue\")` or `.color each= (...)`. Keeps both\nwithout either being silent, at the cost of more surface.\n\n**D. Drop `=` entirely and keep only `|=`.** All updates go through the update operator, and\nrecords come from an explicit `cross` or `for` construct. Smallest core, largest departure.\n\n**E. Rename to a functional-update keyword.** `db with .color = \"red\"`, in the spirit of record\nupdate in ML-family languages. Removes the mutation reading, but adds a keyword and does not by\nitself resolve the cardinality question.\n\nLeaning towards B, because it makes the hazard a type error rather than a naming problem, and\nthe explicit form already exists and reads better. But this interacts with open question 2,\nwhether binary operators are cartesian, zipped, or explicit, so it should not be settled alone.\n\n## Why cardinality-in-the-type is the safety mechanism\n\njq's multi-output semantics produce hazards that are all the same shape, namely the right\nfeature in the wrong position. Measured against a jq implementation:\n\n```\nif (true,false) then \"a\" else \"b\" end   -> [\"a\",\"b\"]       BOTH branches executed\n{} | .a = (1,2)                          -> [{a:1},{a:2}]   assignment forked the world\n{a:1} | .a |= (.,.+10)                   -> [{a:1}]         |= silently took only the first\n(1,2) | (., error(\"boom\"))?              -> [1,2]           `?` truncated with no signal\n```\n\nNone of these is an argument against nondeterminism, because each is genuinely useful\nsomewhere. Forking on assignment is config-matrix expansion. Both-branches is nondeterministic\nchoice. Truncation under `?` is \"take the valid prefix\" of a corrupt stream. What they have in\ncommon is that multiplicity leaked into a position that wanted exactly one value.\n\nMaking cardinality visible turns each into a type error at the point of the mistake:\n","lineStart":1170,"lineEnd":1239,"totalLines":2480,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788217720187,"end":1788217720192}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me look at the section in draft.md about jq operator table, particularly around the cartesian-vs-zip discussion (lines 1310-1340), and understand toylang syntax. Let me also look at how the language represents operators. Let me read a bit around 1150-1320 to understand the operator model and how toylang would express things.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a14fbf4001StF5WFW6fip90E","sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","messageID":"msg_05a14f4a000104DMltckzqfv9D"}}
