{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217722886,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a2d5506086244f06","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md","limit":40,"offset":1290},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1290: ragged data is perfectly meaningful, and jq does it: `[.[][1]]` on `[[1,2],[3]]` yields\n1291: `[2,null]`. What rectangularity buys is that there is no hole. So it does not decide whether the\n1292: operation exists, only whether the result is `Opt`.\n1293: \n1294: **There is one access model, not two.** A tensor is not a second scheme with its own syntax; it is\n1295: the same scheme over a type whose extents happen to be uniform.\n1296: \n1297: **A record is not a dimension.** Its field names are type-level, so iterating it would flatten\n1298: them into positional order and lose them, which is the erasure principle 1 forbids. `to_entries`\n1299: is the written-down version of that crossing, not a workaround for a missing feature. This holds\n1300: whether or not the fields share a type, so it is not a question of finding a common cell type.\n1301: \n1302: ### Consequences to accept\n1303: \n1304: **`Map<K,V>` becomes a distinct type.** A record's keys are known to the compiler; a map's are\n1305: known only to the program. Collapsing them is what forces jq to treat an object as a struct and a\n1306: dictionary at once, which is where its own `.[]` ambiguity comes from. Accepted.\n1307: \n1308: **Five deliberate divergences from jq**, all measured rather than assumed:\n1309: \n1310: | | jq | here |\n1311: |---|---|---|\n1312: | `.[]` on an object | iterates values | error; use `to_entries` |\n1313: | `.[]` at top level | many outputs | one value; there is no stream |\n1314: | `.[9]` out of range | `null` | `Opt` |\n1315: | `null \\| .[0]` | `null` | error |\n1316: | `[.[] + .[]]` | cartesian, `[2,3,3,4]` | not expressible by accident |\n1317: \n1318: ### Settled\n1319: \n1320: **One bracket per dimension**, left to right: `v[3][]`, not `v[3, ]`. This is already jq's\n1321: spelling, and it composes with nesting, which is the general case here; a shape-first\n1322: comma-separated form can be added later as sugar over it if rank-3 tensors turn out to be common.\n1323: \n1324: **Negative indices are a spec.** `[-1]` counts from the end. It collapses, so it was already not\n1325: streamable, and out of range yields `Opt` exactly as a positive index does.\n1326: \n1327: Built, and the ragged case comes out identical to jq: `[[1,2],[3]][][1]` is `[2,null]` on all\n1328: three backends, which is `[.[][1]]` there. Keeping one dimension and collapsing the next is the\n1329: same query, arrived at from dimensions rather than from streams.\n\n(Showing lines 1290-1329 of 2480. Use offset=1330 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"ragged data is perfectly meaningful, and jq does it: `[.[][1]]` on `[[1,2],[3]]` yields\n`[2,null]`. What rectangularity buys is that there is no hole. So it does not decide whether the\noperation exists, only whether the result is `Opt`.\n\n**There is one access model, not two.** A tensor is not a second scheme with its own syntax; it is\nthe same scheme over a type whose extents happen to be uniform.\n\n**A record is not a dimension.** Its field names are type-level, so iterating it would flatten\nthem into positional order and lose them, which is the erasure principle 1 forbids. `to_entries`\nis the written-down version of that crossing, not a workaround for a missing feature. This holds\nwhether or not the fields share a type, so it is not a question of finding a common cell type.\n\n### Consequences to accept\n\n**`Map<K,V>` becomes a distinct type.** A record's keys are known to the compiler; a map's are\nknown only to the program. Collapsing them is what forces jq to treat an object as a struct and a\ndictionary at once, which is where its own `.[]` ambiguity comes from. Accepted.\n\n**Five deliberate divergences from jq**, all measured rather than assumed:\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-169/draft.md","text":"ragged data is perfectly meaningful, and jq does it: `[.[][1]]` on `[[1,2],[3]]` yields\n`[2,null]`. What rectangularity buys is that there is no hole. So it does not decide whether the\noperation exists, only whether the result is `Opt`.\n\n**There is one access model, not two.** A tensor is not a second scheme with its own syntax; it is\nthe same scheme over a type whose extents happen to be uniform.\n\n**A record is not a dimension.** Its field names are type-level, so iterating it would flatten\nthem into positional order and lose them, which is the erasure principle 1 forbids. `to_entries`\nis the written-down version of that crossing, not a workaround for a missing feature. This holds\nwhether or not the fields share a type, so it is not a question of finding a common cell type.\n\n### Consequences to accept\n\n**`Map<K,V>` becomes a distinct type.** A record's keys are known to the compiler; a map's are\nknown only to the program. Collapsing them is what forces jq to treat an object as a struct and a\ndictionary at once, which is where its own `.[]` ambiguity comes from. Accepted.\n\n**Five deliberate divergences from jq**, all measured rather than assumed:\n\n| | jq | here |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.[]` on an object | iterates values | error; use `to_entries` |\n| `.[]` at top level | many outputs | one value; there is no stream |\n| `.[9]` out of range | `null` | `Opt` |\n| `null \\| .[0]` | `null` | error |\n| `[.[] + .[]]` | cartesian, `[2,3,3,4]` | not expressible by accident |\n\n### Settled\n\n**One bracket per dimension**, left to right: `v[3][]`, not `v[3, ]`. This is already jq's\nspelling, and it composes with nesting, which is the general case here; a shape-first\ncomma-separated form can be added later as sugar over it if rank-3 tensors turn out to be common.\n\n**Negative indices are a spec.** `[-1]` counts from the end. It collapses, so it was already not\nstreamable, and out of range yields `Opt` exactly as a positive index does.\n\nBuilt, and the ragged case comes out identical to jq: `[[1,2],[3]][][1]` is `[2,null]` on all\nthree backends, which is `[.[][1]]` there. Keeping one dimension and collapsing the next is the\nsame query, arrived at from dimensions rather than from streams.","lineStart":1290,"lineEnd":1329,"totalLines":2480,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788217722873,"end":1788217722884}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me understand the \"here\" column in the jq comparison table — what toylang currently does. Let me look at the operator comparison table around line 1310.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a15060e0012Vg57AArDASXw3","sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","messageID":"msg_05a14fda0001e0Gjsa1FLpspz0"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788217722898,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","part":{"id":"prt_05a15080d001VDN16s2ivQ5lK8","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"6a598f8a24b34371f9755203ce4bf243b87859cd","messageID":"msg_05a14fda0001e0Gjsa1FLpspz0","sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":26589,"input":1367,"output":93,"reasoning":41,"cache":{"write":0,"read":25088}},"cost":0.000514383}}
