{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217743213,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"webfetch","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b6bdba2aef406db4","state":{"status":"error","input":{"url":"https://jqlang.github.io/jq/manual/v1.7/","format":"markdown"},"error":"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.","time":{"start":1788217743205,"end":1788217743211}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good understanding of toylang syntax. It uses `|` pipes, `select`, `map`-like via `select(...)`, `.[]` projection, records with braces, named functions. `Vec<T>` type.\n\nLet me understand how toylang currently does operators and how it would express zip/cross. The question is about binary operators over two Vecs. In jq, `+` on arrays concatenates, but other operators (like `+` on streams from `.[]`, or `.` combine) are cartesian.\n\nActually wait — in jq, the cartesian product comes from streams, not from arrays. `[.[] + .[]]` — here `.[]` produces a stream, and `+` applied to two streams is the cartesian product. In toylang there are no streams; everything is a Vec. So the question is what a binary operator like `+` (non-concat, e.g. `<`, `>`, `==`, or arithmetic) means when both operands are Vecs.\n\nLet me now research real jq idioms that rely on the cartesian default. The classic jq cartesian idiom is `.[] + .[]` for cross products. Let me collect real idioms from jq docs and cookbook:\n\n1. **Cross product / cartesian join**: `[.users[] as $u | .roles[] as $r | {user: $u, role: $r}]` — but this uses explicit `as`. Actually the pure cartesian-operator form is `[.[] + .[]]` (elementwise cross of two arrays' elements). Common in jq cookbook: e.g. `[.x[] + .y[]]`? Hmm.\n\nLet me think about the actual jq idioms that use cartesian operator default:\n- `reduce` over streams\n- `[.[] + .[]]` — cartesian sum\n- Matrix multiply via `[.A[][] * .B[][] ]`? \n\nLet me be careful and use real jq docs examples. Known jq cookbook idioms:\n\n1. Cartesian product: `[.users[] as $u | .roles[] as $r | [$u.name, $r.name]]` — explicit, not operator-default. But there's also the terse `.[] + .[]` form.\n\nActually, the jq manual example for cartesian product of arrays: \n`[1,2,3] | .[] | [., .]` etc.\n\nLet me recall jq's `combinations` builtin: `combinations` produces cartesian product of arrays in input. And `combinations(n)`.\n\nThe key cartesian-operator-default idiom in jq: `.[] * .[]` or `[.[] + .[]]`.\n\nLet me look at jq docs real examples:\n- The jq manual \"map and join\": `[range(3) as $x | range(3) as $y | [$x, $y]]` cartesian via nested range.\n\nActually, the most canonical jq idiom relying on cartesian default of operators over streams is computing a cartesian product via operator: e.g. `[.[] as $x | .[] as $y | $x * $y]`.\n\nBut for operators directly: `reduce .[] as $item (0; . + $item)`. \n\nHmm. Let me think about what operators do in jq with cartesian default:\n\n- `+` on two streams: `(1,2) + (10,20)` → `11,21,12,22` (cartesian). This is the canonical one.\n- `*` similarly.\n\nReal-world use: computing all pairwise comparisons, e.g. `[.[] * .[]]` for pairwise products.\n\nLet me look at actual jq docs. Let me search online for jq cookbook cartesian. Let me use webfetch.\n\nActually, I should be efficient. Let me gather real jq idioms. Key real idioms that rely on cartesian default:\n\n1. **Cartesian product of two streams via operator**: `[.prices[] * .quantities[]]` — no, that's not standard. \n\nLet me get the real jq manual examples. The jq manual has:\n- `[.[] + 1]` — but that's scalar + stream, broadcast, not cartesian.\n- Cartesian requires both sides streams.\n\njq cookbook (https://github.com/stedolan/jq/wiki/Cookbook) has:\n- \"cartesian product\" example.\n\nLet me fetch it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a155408001feap3PaJw35mlt","sessionID":"ses_fa5eb6461ffeMsD7uLAy4rsiUF","messageID":"msg_05a151a06001fBw723SgtM9